        "**The problem.** You need to enrich 500 leads. Each API call takes about a second, almost all of it *waiting* for the other server. One after another, that’s over 8 minutes of your program mostly doing nothing.",
        "**Async** lets one program start a call and, while it waits, start others. **The analogy:** a waiter doesn’t stand at one table until the food arrives; they take other orders meanwhile.",
        "- `async def` defines a function that can pause.\n- `await` marks the pause: “wait for this, and let other work run meanwhile”.\n- The [[event loop]] is the waiter, switching between paused tasks. `asyncio.run(main())` starts it.\n- `asyncio.gather(...)` runs several tasks and waits for all of them.",
        "**Limit how many run at once.** That’s your [[concurrency]]. Firing 500 requests at once will hit the [[rate limit]]. `asyncio.Semaphore(5)` lets at most 5 run at a time.",
        "**The trap.** Async only helps while waiting on the network or disk. A blocking call inside an async function, like `time.sleep()` or `requests.get()`, freezes *every* task, because the waiter is stuck too. Use async versions: `await asyncio.sleep()` and `httpx.AsyncClient`. Heavy calculation doesn’t get faster with async either.",
        "**When plain code is better:** a script making a handful of calls, an API that only allows a few requests a second anyway, or a flow you’re still figuring out. Normal (sync) code is easier to read and debug. Reach for async when waiting is clearly the bottleneck. You’ll also need to read it: FastAPI, in the FastAPI module, is async.",
